<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter10_access-control-friends-and-protected">
    <title>Access Control Revisited: Friends and <c>protected</c></title>

    <p>
        In Chapter 5, we used <c>private</c> members to protect an object's
        internal state and <c>public</c> member functions to define its
        interface. C++ also provides <c>protected</c> access and friendship.
        These features give a class more design choices, but they should be
        used deliberately because they widen access to implementation details.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_access-control-levels">
        <title>Three Access Levels</title>

        <p>
            An access specifier controls which code may name a member directly.
            The following table describes the usual rules for a class and a
            class derived from it. Inheritance will be studied fully later.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter10_access-levels">
            <title>Access control in a class hierarchy</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="22%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="27%"/>
                <col width="26%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Specifier</cell>
                    <cell>Member functions of the class</cell>
                    <cell>Member functions of a derived class</cell>
                    <cell>Unrelated outside code</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>public</c></cell>
                    <cell>Can access</cell>
                    <cell>Can access</cell>
                    <cell>Can access</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>protected</c></cell>
                    <cell>Can access</cell>
                    <cell>Can access</cell>
                    <cell>Cannot access directly</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>private</c></cell>
                    <cell>Can access</cell>
                    <cell>Cannot access directly</cell>
                    <cell>Cannot access directly</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            A <c>protected</c> member is hidden from ordinary outside code but
            is available to member functions of a derived class. This can be
            useful when a base class deliberately designs an implementation
            extension point. However, private data plus protected or public
            member functions often provides stronger control than protected
            data itself.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_friend-functions">
        <title>Friend Functions</title>

        <p>
            A <term>friend function</term> is not a member function, but a class
            may grant it permission to access private and protected members. The
            class places a friend declaration inside its definition. The friend
            function is then defined outside the class like an ordinary
            non-member function.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_friend-temperature">
            <title>A friend function compares two private temperatures</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_friend-temperature"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Temperature {
private:
    double celsius;

public:
    explicit Temperature(double starting_celsius)
        : celsius{starting_celsius} {
    }

    friend bool have_same_temperature(
        const Temperature& first,
        const Temperature& second
    );
};

bool have_same_temperature(
    const Temperature& first,
    const Temperature& second
) {
    return first.celsius == second.celsius;
}

int main() {
    Temperature morning{20.0};
    Temperature afternoon{20.0};

    std::cout << have_same_temperature(morning, afternoon) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            <c>have_same_temperature</c> is not called with the dot operator
            because it is not a member function. Its friend declaration gives
            it permission to read the private <c>celsius</c> members of both
            objects. Without that declaration, the function could not access
            them directly.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_friend-classes">
        <title>Friend Classes</title>

        <p>
            A class can also declare another class as a friend. Every member
            function of the friend class may then access the granting class's
            private and protected members. Friend classes can support closely
            related components, but they create a strong dependency between the
            two classes.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter10_friend-class"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
class AccountInspector;

class StudentAccount {
private:
    double balance = 0.0;

    friend class AccountInspector;
};

class AccountInspector {
public:
    double read_balance(const StudentAccount& account) const {
        return account.balance;
    }
};
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Friendship is not inherited, not mutual, and not transitive. If
            <c>StudentAccount</c> makes <c>AccountInspector</c> a friend,
            <c>AccountInspector</c> does not automatically make
            <c>StudentAccount</c> a friend. A friend of one class is not
            automatically a friend of a derived class or of another class.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_protected-members">
        <title>Using <c>protected</c> with a Derived Class</title>

        <p>
            The <c>protected</c> specifier is mainly useful in class hierarchies.
            The following small example previews the idea. A complete study of
            inheritance and polymorphism comes later.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_protected-account">
            <title>A derived class can use a protected member</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_protected-account"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Account {
protected:
    double balance = 0.0;
};

class SavingsAccount : public Account {
public:
    void add_interest() {
        balance += 5.0;
    }

    double get_balance() const {
        return balance;
    }
};

int main() {
    SavingsAccount account;

    account.add_interest();
    std::cout << account.get_balance() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            <c>SavingsAccount</c> can use <c>balance</c> because it is a derived
            class. Code in <c>main</c> cannot write
            <c>account.balance</c> directly because <c>protected</c> is not
            public. The public member function provides the outside interface.
        </p>

        <p>
            There is one additional C++ rule to remember. A derived-class
            member function may access a protected member through the current
            derived object or through an object whose type is that derived class
            or one of its subclasses. It may not use the protected member through
            an arbitrary base-class object.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter10_protected-object-rule"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
class SavingsAccount : public Account {
public:
    void add_bonus(SavingsAccount& other) {
        other.balance += 5.0;  // allowed
    }

    void audit_base_account(Account& base) {
        // base.balance += 5.0;  // error: base is only an Account
    }
};
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The rule protects the base-class part of an unrelated
            <c>Account</c> object from being reached through a derived-class
            interface. In the preceding example, <c>other</c> is known to be a
            <c>SavingsAccount</c>, but <c>base</c> could refer to a standalone
            <c>Account</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Prefer private data when possible. A protected data member exposes
            more of the base class's implementation to derived classes, which
            can make future changes harder. Protected member functions can offer
            a narrower extension point.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_friends-codelens">
        <title>Tracing a Friend Function</title>

        <p>
            Step through the comparison. The friend function is outside the
            class, but the class has granted it access to private state.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter10_friend_function"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Temperature%20%7B%0Aprivate%3A%0A%20%20%20%20double%20celsius%3B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Temperature%28double%20value%29%20%3A%20celsius%7Bvalue%7D%20%7B%7D%0A%20%20%20%20friend%20bool%20same%28const%20Temperature%26%20first%2C%20const%20Temperature%26%20second%29%3B%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Abool%20same%28const%20Temperature%26%20first%2C%20const%20Temperature%26%20second%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20return%20first.celsius%20%3D%3D%20second.celsius%3B%0A%7D%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Temperature%20first%7B21.0%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Temperature%20second%7B21.0%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20same%28first%2C%20second%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=560&amp;codeDivWidth=450&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;mode=display&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="4:3">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter10_access_exercise-table">
        <statement>
            <p>Which access specifier allows a derived class to use a member while ordinary outside code cannot use it directly?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>protected</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Derived-class member functions may access protected members, while unrelated outside code may not.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>private</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Private members are not directly available to derived-class member functions.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>public</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Public members are also directly available to unrelated outside code.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>friend</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p><c>friend</c> grants a special relationship; it is not an access-specifier category.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_friend_exercise-access">
        <statement>
            <p>Why can <c>have_same_temperature</c> read the private <c>celsius</c> member?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>Temperature</c> declared the function as a friend.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A friend declaration grants the named non-member function special access.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Every non-member function can read private data.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Private members are normally inaccessible to non-member functions.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The function is secretly a static member.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Friend functions are independent non-member functions. They are not static members, even though neither function type receives an implicit <c>this</c> pointer.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The member becomes public whenever two objects are compared.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Access does not change during a comparison.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_friend_parsons">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the lines to declare and define a friend comparison function.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>class Temperature {</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>private: double celsius;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>friend bool same(const Temperature&amp; first, const Temperature&amp; second);</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>};</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>bool same(const Temperature&amp; first, const Temperature&amp; second) {</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>    return first.celsius == second.celsius;</cline></block>
            <block order="7"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_friend_exercise-properties">
        <statement>
            <p>Which statement about friendship is correct?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Friendship is granted explicitly and is not automatically mutual or transitive.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A friend relationship gives the named class or function special access only as specified.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>If class A is a friend of class B, B is automatically a friend of A.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Friendship is not automatically mutual.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>If A is a friend of B and B is a friend of C, A is automatically a friend of C.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Friendship is not transitive.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Every derived class automatically inherits all friendship relationships.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Friendship is not inherited automatically.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_protected_exercise">
        <statement>
            <p>Why can <c>SavingsAccount::add_interest</c> change <c>balance</c> in the example?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>SavingsAccount</c> is derived from <c>Account</c>, and <c>balance</c> is protected.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Protected members are available to derived-class member functions.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>All data members are accessible through inheritance.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Private base-class members are not directly accessible to derived classes.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>balance</c> is public because it has a default value.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A default member initializer does not change access control.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>protected</c> means public only inside <c>main</c>.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Protected access is intended for the class and its derived classes, not unrelated outside code.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</section>
